        "Non-systems items apply across every patient population. They test whether you can select and fit equipment, choose and apply modalities safely, protect patients and staff, act within legal and ethical boundaries, and interpret research. These items often look simple but contain one detail that changes the answer.",
        "<b>Equipment and devices.</b> A single-point or quad cane is held on the side opposite the painful or weak limb and advanced with that limb; height is usually set at the level of the wrist crease or greater trochanter, with about 20-30 degrees of elbow flexion. On stairs with crutches or a cane, remember 'up with the good, down with the bad': the stronger leg leads up, and the weaker leg leads down with the device. Wheelchair fit matters: a seat that is too narrow increases pressure over the greater trochanters, a seat that is too deep pressures the back of the knees, and footrests set too high increase pressure on the ischial tuberosities.",
        "<b>Modalities.</b> Therapeutic ultrasound at 1 MHz penetrates deeper tissue, while 3 MHz heats more superficial tissue. Conventional (high-rate) TENS uses high frequency and short pulse duration at a sensory level and works mainly through gate control. Iontophoresis drives charged medication with like-charge repulsion; dexamethasone sodium phosphate is negatively charged and delivered from the negative electrode (cathode). Common cryotherapy contraindications include cold urticaria, cryoglobulinemia, and Raynaud phenomenon. Electrical stimulation is generally contraindicated over a demand-type pacemaker or the carotid sinus and over the trunk or pelvis during pregnancy. Thermal agents require intact sensation and circulation and careful skin checks.",
        "<b>Safety and protection.</b> Standard precautions apply to every patient. Contact precautions apply to organisms such as MRSA and C. difficile; for C. difficile, wash hands with soap and water because alcohol-based rub does not reliably kill its spores. Airborne precautions, including an N95 respirator and a negative-pressure room, apply to tuberculosis. Droplet precautions apply to influenza. Use proper body mechanics, gait belts, and enough staff for transfers. If a patient starts to fall during gait, guide the patient down while protecting the head rather than trying to hold the full weight.",
        "<b>Professional responsibilities.</b> PTs are responsible for evaluation, diagnosis, prognosis, plan of care, and discharge; PTAs work within the plan; aides perform non-skilled tasks. Suspected abuse or neglect of a child, elder, or dependent adult must be reported according to state law. A colleague who appears impaired at work is a patient safety issue and must be reported through the appropriate channel. Informed consent requires disclosure of the nature, risks, benefits, and alternatives of care, and patients may refuse.",
        "<b>Research and EBP.</b> High sensitivity means few false negatives, so a negative result helps rule out (SnNout). High specificity means few false positives, so a positive result helps rule in (SpPin). Likelihood ratios show how much a test changes probability: a positive LR above about 10 or a negative LR below about 0.1 causes large shifts. Number needed to treat is <span class='formula'>1 / absolute risk reduction</span>. Cohort studies best answer prognosis questions; randomized controlled trials best answer intervention questions. The minimal detectable change (MDC) is the smallest change beyond measurement error, while the minimal clinically important difference (MCID) is the smallest change patients find meaningful.",
        "<b>Worked example.</b> 'A trial reports that 20% of patients in usual care had a recurrence of low back pain within a year, compared with 10% in an exercise group. How many patients must receive the exercise program to prevent one recurrence?' Absolute risk reduction = 20% - 10% = 10%, or 0.10. NNT = 1 / 0.10 = 10. The trap answers are 2 (using relative risk reduction of 50%) and 20 (misreading the control rate)."
